WEBVTT

resistance-02-1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:06.510
Previously: anxiety has no object. It
grows out of ‘wanting to become.’

resistance-02-2
00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:13.669
This time, we take a step back: where
exactly is the thing that makes us feel

resistance-02-3
00:00:13.669 --> 00:00:14.320
secure?

resistance-02-4
00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:20.970
Episode 2: Can Security Only Be ‘Felt’?

resistance-02-5
00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:28.009
Far from home, she works late into the
night. When she is exhausted, she pulls

resistance-02-6
00:00:28.009 --> 00:00:29.260
out a photograph.

resistance-02-7
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:33.723
Her grandmother’s kitchen. Looking at it,
she feels: I am still me. I have

resistance-02-8
00:00:33.723 --> 00:00:35.110
somewhere to return to.

resistance-02-9
00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:43.137
Pause for a moment: the scene in which
you feel most secure—is it happening now,

resistance-02-10
00:00:43.137 --> 00:00:47.050
or is it a memory or an imagined scene?

resistance-02-11
00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:54.534
She wants to make that security real. At
Lunar New Year, she returns to her

resistance-02-12
00:00:54.534 --> 00:00:55.300
hometown.

resistance-02-13
00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:59.980
The old house has been demolished. A
convenience store stands there now.

resistance-02-14
00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:06.825
The security in the photograph has
nowhere to take root in reality. This is

resistance-02-15
00:01:06.825 --> 00:01:08.650
where unease begins.

resistance-02-16
00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:13.210
There are two kinds of material that make
us feel secure.

resistance-02-17
00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:16.710
Memory: my grandmother’s kitchen, my past
self.

resistance-02-18
00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:20.830
Imagination: a year from now, I can give
a speech onstage in Japanese.

resistance-02-19
00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:26.829
One points to the past, the other to the
future. They share one thing: neither is

resistance-02-20
00:01:26.829 --> 00:01:28.310
happening right now.

resistance-02-21
00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:31.555
Berenskoetter 2014, pp. 270–272: memories
and visions of the future both provide

resistance-02-22
00:01:31.555 --> 00:01:33.340
material for a subject’s identity
narrative.

resistance-02-23
00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.040
This series’ proposition is:

resistance-02-24
00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:42.470
Ontological security can only be felt
‘reflexively’; it can never be realized.

resistance-02-25
00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:48.260
People can feel secure, but they can
never ‘be’ secure.

resistance-02-26
00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:52.610
This series’ proposition

resistance-02-27
00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:57.020
Let us draw this as a circle:

resistance-02-28
00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:02.020
Reflexive security: memory and
imagination make us feel secure.

resistance-02-29
00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:06.400
Trying to realize it: hold on to the
past, or bring the future into being.

resistance-02-30
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.820
Existential anxiety: an uncertain reality
contradicts that feeling of security.

resistance-02-31
00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:17.479
Return to memory and imagination: when
realization is blocked, seek comfort in

resistance-02-32
00:02:17.479 --> 00:02:18.930
that material again.

resistance-02-33
00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:23.768
The moment realization is blocked, we are
thrown back into reality. Then we look at

resistance-02-34
00:02:23.768 --> 00:02:26.450
that photograph again. The circle starts
anew.

resistance-02-35
00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.137
‘Thrown’ is borrowed from Heidegger’s
account of Dasein (Being and Time, 1962

resistance-02-36
00:02:29.137 --> 00:02:31.650
English translation, p. 174); the cycle
itself is the author’s argument.

resistance-02-37
00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:36.560
What does this have to do with
resistance?

resistance-02-38
00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.808
One person’s security comes from the
past—from ‘who I already am.’ He wants to

resistance-02-39
00:02:40.808 --> 00:02:41.570
hold on to it.

resistance-02-40
00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:45.348
Another’s security comes from the
future—from ‘who I ought to be.’ She

resistance-02-41
00:02:45.348 --> 00:02:46.590
wants to strive for it.

resistance-02-42
00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:54.273
Both are trying to make the security they
feel real. But in reality, there is only

resistance-02-43
00:02:54.273 --> 00:02:55.210
one chair.

resistance-02-44
00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:57.870
Next time:

resistance-02-45
00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:04.610
Those Who Hold On, Those Who Strive: Why
Are Both Anxious?

resistance-02-46
00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:11.810
This is UjiBricks.
